WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.323
I was born deaf.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.863
My parents found out when I was five.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.813
I did go through speech therapy.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.890
I can speak some.

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.850
Hi.

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.313
Like that as an example.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.910
Around 5% of the world's population

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.433
is affected by some form of hearing loss.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.280
While hearing aids are helpful for some

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.780
they're ineffective or
only partially effective

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:54.830
for many deaf people.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:58.060
And cochlear implants are expensive

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.013
and require an invasive surgery.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:07.390
Recently, inventors around
the world have been working on

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:10.413
new ways of letting deaf
people experience sound.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:27.800
Something we don't remember

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.540
but all of us had to
learn how to use our ears.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.870
So when we were born,

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.280
our brains didn't know how to interpret

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.230
all these volleys of spikes coming in.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.890
And eventually it became

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.483
just the perceptual experience of hearing.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:42.720
And this is exactly what happens

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.140
to people with the wristband.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:44.973
They start off saying,

00:01:44.973 --> 00:01:47.090
"Oh, there's something
buzzing on my skin."

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.199
And then after a while
they simply hear with it.

00:01:53.493 --> 00:01:54.326
Baby.

00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:40.350
What the brain is really good at doing

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.240
is taking in information,

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.070
and putting together a story
of what's going on out there.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.360
But the surprising part is,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.440
it doesn't care how it
gets that information.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.040
So we're used to eyes and
ears and nose and mouth

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:53.430
and fingertips and so on.

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:55.720
But it turns out that you can
get the information in there

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.970
a different way, and the
brain will figure it out.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:01.490
This is what's known as
sensory substitution.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:02.750
So in my neuroscience lab,

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.040
we got interested in this
topic of sensory substitution

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.320
and whether we could do that
for people who are deaf.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.960
So we built a vest that
has vibratory motors on it.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.830
So this is the vest that we built.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.810
And the vest capture sound and turns that

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.981
into patterns of vibration
on the skin of the torso.

00:04:22.981 --> 00:04:23.814
Sleep.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:27.783
Work.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:32.120
And deaf people could get so good at this

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.729
that they were able to
understand spoken words.

00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:37.562
Where.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.600
So, we ended up shrinking
that down over time

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.990
to a wrist band.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.810
And it's listening to the world

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.840
and then it has vibratory
motors all down here.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.430
And this is spread in a spectrum

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.950
so that you're actually feeling
the sounds from low to high.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:55.920
Which is exactly what your inner ear does.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.000
Your inner ear picks up
on sounds of the world

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.260
breaks it into low to high frequencies

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.650
and ships that off to the brain.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:02.500
We're doing the same thing

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.900
but we've transferred the
inner ear to the skin.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.130
So that was the car engine starting.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:13.900
And I could feel,

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:17.300
I could feel the vibration
on my skin of the engine.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.810
And of course, since it's mapped
from low to high frequency,

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.100
I feel it go up and down.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.710
I feel it spatially

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.333
as well as the amplitude of it on my skin.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.300
Phillip was one of our first participants

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.610
when we were testing
this out some years ago.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.790
And so Phillip actually wore
a very early prototype of this

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.963
and it was very life-changing too.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.830
They gave me an example of
the first product they had.

00:05:48.830 --> 00:05:51.860
And I started crying when I first used it.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.080
It was great.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.300
I was able to feel and
understand so much stuff

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.440
that was happening around me.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:00.550
I know when I have it on,
I do have this connection.

00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:02.550
I feel more connected with my family.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:03.930
I'm able to recognize words

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:07.070
like when they say I love
you and things like that.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:09.990
I'm also able to feel
emotion in people's voices.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.790
And I think we should all have that.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:14.910
Okay, so, Phillip, you ready?

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.340
I'm gonna play some sounds

00:06:16.340 --> 00:06:19.023
and then you see if you can identify them.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:24.650
Knocking on the door.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.850
Very good. Here we go.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.560
Oh, dog barking.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:30.393
Yep.

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:34.640
Oh, that's the car horn.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:35.833
Yeah, very good.

00:06:41.754 --> 00:06:45.087
Oh, yeah, that's definitely the piano.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:47.223
I love music.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.080
Without this, I'd have to,
you turn up music really loud

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:56.530
and put my hands on everything
to feel the vibrations.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.620
I don't wanna have to touch everything,

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.823
especially with COVID.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.600
But with the wristband,
I can feel everything.

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:29.480
Okay, so, now we stepped out into the road

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.870
to test the wristband in the wild

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.000
and to see if Philip can tell

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:36.033
when there's a car coming down the road.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:43.960
Yeah, yeah.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.110
Okay, let's see if we
can hear another one.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:52.513
Yeah.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.920
This guy is feeling
real-time feed of data.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.600
The question that I've been
asking as neuroscientist is

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.380
can we actually make it so that

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.420
we're picking up on
different kinds of signals

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.600
and experiencing a new kind of reality.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:08.690
We brought in a blind participant
and put the vest on him

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.520
and he could tell where everybody was

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.930
by feeling where people are around him.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.190
We have 70 other projects
in development right now

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.280
for feeding in new kinds
of senses to people.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:23.810
So not merely substitution,
but addition of senses.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:27.940
So for example, being able
to detect infrared light

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:32.170
or electrical fields or the
movement of the stock market

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.960
or Twitter sentiment

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:35.840
or what's going on with your drone

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:36.850
as you're flying it around.
Where you're actually

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.670
feeling the pitch, yaw, roll orientation

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.390
and heading of your drone on your skin.

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.540
Whatever you plug in, the
brain will figure it out.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:47.000
And it turns out if
you plug in new senses,

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:48.330
as long as it's relevant,

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:51.540
the brain will figure out
what to do with those senses.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:52.910
All the information is sitting there

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.360
but normally we're blind to it.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.410
And it is possible to take
in this kind of information

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.633
and add it to our repertoire
of what we consider reality.

